     "text": "**My recommendation: negotiate.** The offer is solid but not great. It beats what you make now by only about 5\u201315% in year one, and you'd be giving up a job you're happy in. Google reached out to you, so you can ask for more without much risk.\n\n## 1. The numbers\n\n**Google offer, assuming no raises and the stock price stays flat:**\n\n| Year | Cash (base + target bonus) | Equity vesting | Total | Without any refreshes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | $282k | $270k | **$552k** | $552k |\n| 2 | $282k | $332.5k | **$614.5k** | $552k |\n| 3 | $282k | $395k | **$677k** | $552k |\n| 4 | $282k | $457.5k | **$739.5k** | $552k |\n| 5+ | $282k | ~$250k | **~$532k** | $282k |\n\nThe recruiter's illustration checks out: each $250k refresh adds $62.5k a year in vesting.\n\n**Meta, if you stay:** your run rate is about $526k. But your actual next 12 months are about **$476k** ($240k base + $36k bonus + $200k RSUs), plus whatever new refreshes Meta gives you.\n\n**What the comparison really shows:**\n- **Year one:** Google pays about $26k to $76k more (+5% to +15%).\n- **Years 2\u20134 look big, but that's mostly temporary.** The growth comes from the new-hire grant stacking on top of refreshes. Meta also gives refreshes, which aren't guaranteed either. That's why your Meta pay is a steady ~$250k a year in equity. By year 5, Google settles to about $532k, almost the same as your Meta run rate today.\n- **The fair comparison is refreshes vs. refreshes.** Comparing Google with refreshes against Meta without them is misleading. Overall, the true premium of this offer is roughly the new-hire grant minus what you'd forfeit at Meta.\n\n## 2. Weak spots in the offer\n- **The base is a pay cut.** $235k vs. your $240k, even though the posted range goes up to $271k.\n- **There's no sign-on bonus,** even though you'd likely give up Meta equity and a partial year's bonus when you leave.\n- **There's a refresh gap.** Your first Google refresh probably won't start vesting until year 2.\n- **You're taking on switching risk:** a new team, a new review cycle, and starting over on building a track record.\n- **Less of it is certain than it looks.** The bonus depends on performance, and the equity value depends on the stock price. Only the base and the number of shares granted are fixed.\n\n## 3. What to ask for\n\n| Item | Ask | Realistic outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base | **$255k** | $245\u2013250k |\n| New-hire RSUs | **$1.35M** ($337.5k/yr) | $1.2\u20131.25M |\n| Sign-on bonus | **$75k** (to cover forfeited Meta pay and the refresh gap) | $40\u201360k |\n\nIf you got the realistic outcome, year one would be about **$640\u2013660k**, roughly 25\u201335% above your current pay. That's a good result for someone who wasn't looking. Equity and sign-on are usually where Google has the most room, so focus your push there. Base has the least.\n\n**A script you can adapt:**\n> \"I'm excited about the role, and I wasn't looking \u2014 I'm happy at Meta, so I'd need this to be a clear step up. Right now the base is below my current salary, and I'd be forfeiting unvested equity and a partial-year bonus. To make the move, I'd need a base of $255k, a new-hire grant of $1.35M, and a $75k sign-on. If we can get there, I'm ready to sign.\"\n\nDon't bluff about other offers. \"I'm happy where I am\" is honest, and it's strong leverage.\n\n## 4. Before you decide\n1. **Add up all your unvested Meta equity,** not just the next 12 months. That's what you'd walk away from, and the Google grant should clearly beat it.\n2. **Get the vesting details in writing:** the schedule (the recruiter's math assumes even 25% a year), the vesting frequency, and how the dollar value converts to shares (usually an average stock price, so timing matters).\n3. **Check your Meta bonus timing.** Leaving just before a payout can cost you tens of thousands of dollars, so time your start date around it.\n4. **Set your walk-away number now.** For example: \"I'll move only for at least $600k in fairly certain year-one pay.\" If Google barely moves, turning it down is completely reasonable. You'd be staying in a job you like at almost the same long-run pay.\n\n**Bottom line:** don't accept as-is, and don't walk away yet. Make one clear, confident ask for the items above. Then accept if they get close to your number, and stay at Meta if they don't.",
