     "text": "**Yes, I'd take it, as long as the written terms check out and you're comfortable leaving a team you like.** This is a strong final offer, and pushing Google again would likely gain nothing and could cost you some goodwill.\n\n## Why it's a good result\n\n**Year one:**\n\n| | Google | Meta (stay) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base | $240k | $240k |\n| Bonus (target) | $48k | ~$36k |\n| RSUs vesting | $270k | ~$200k |\n| Sign-on | $200k | \u2014 |\n| **Total** | **~$758k** | **~$476k** |\n\nThat's about **$280k more (+59%)** in year one. Even without the sign-on, Google pays about $82k more.\n\n**How it compares to what I suggested asking for:**\n- **Base:** You're now at $240k, matching your Meta base. My realistic target was $245\u2013250k.\n- **Equity:** It didn't move.\n- **Sign-on:** $200k is far above the $40\u201360k I expected.\n\nOver 4 years, this comes out about the same as my \"realistic outcome\" scenario, roughly $2.43M before refreshes. But more of it arrives early and in cash. Cash doesn't depend on Google's stock price, so that makes this version slightly better, not worse.\n\n**Over 4 years, comparing refreshes to refreshes:** If Google refreshes at about $250k a year and Meta's pay holds at about $526k a year, Google comes out ahead by roughly **$500k\u2013700k** over 4 years. Most of that lead comes in years 1\u20134. By year 5, the two converge to about $530k each, as I noted before.\n\n## Things to know before signing\n1. **Year two will feel like a pay cut.** Pay drops from about $758k to about $560\u2013620k. That's still above Meta, but plan your spending around the year-two number, not year one.\n2. **Sign-on clawback.** Find out whether you'd have to repay it if you leave within 12 or 24 months, and whether repayment is full or prorated. Also ask whether it's paid as a lump sum or in installments.\n3. **Taxes on the sign-on.** It's usually withheld at flat supplemental rates (22% federal plus California's rate). That's probably below your actual tax bracket, so set money aside to avoid a surprise at tax time.\n4. **Get it all in writing:** the RSU vesting schedule and frequency, and how $1.08M converts to shares (which stock price and date are used).\n5. **Time your start date.** Check whether a Meta RSU vest or bonus payout is coming up in the next few weeks. Asking to push your start date back is a normal request, not a renegotiation, and it could be worth tens of thousands of dollars.\n\n## Should you ask Meta to counter?\nYou can, but only if you'd actually stay for the right number. You'd need to show them the offer, and that changes how people see you there even if you stay. Meta matching about $280k of year-one value is a big ask. If you're already leaning toward Google, I'd skip it.\n\n## The real remaining question\nOn money alone, this is a clear yes, and you said compensation matters a lot to you. What's left is non-financial: Google's team, manager, scope, and growth path compared with a job you're happy in. If Google's role is at least as good on those points, sign it. If it's clearly worse, the extra ~$125\u2013175k a year averaged over 4 years is what you'd be giving up by staying, and only you can decide whether that's worth it.",
